L000254	\subsection*{Steady-state phonon distribution}
L000255	\label{app_steady}
L000256	
L000257	Regarding the steady-state phonon distribution of the bosonic degree of freedom of the trapped-ion system, described by Eq.~(1) in the main text \cite{so2025fT} with $[H,\rho]=0$, we can consider the diagonal component of the density matrix $p_n=\braket{n|\rho|n}$, which yields:
L000258	\begin{eqnarray}
L000259	    \frac{\partial p_n}{\partial t}&=&(\gamma_c+\gamma_h)(p_{n+1}(n+1)-p_n n) \nonumber \\
L000260	    &\quad&+\;\gamma_h(p_{n-1} n - p_{n}(n+1)),
L000261	\end{eqnarray}
L000262	where $\frac{\partial p_n}{\partial t}=0$. This equation gives the detailed-balance recurrence for $n\geq1$:
L000263	\begin{equation}
L000264	    (\gamma_c+\gamma_h) p_n = \gamma_h p_{n-1},
L000265	\end{equation}
L000266	resulting in:
L000267	\begin{equation}
L000268	    \frac{p_{n}}{p_{n-1}}=\frac{\gamma_h/\gamma_c}{\gamma_h/\gamma_c+1}=\frac{n_{\rm ss}}{n_{\rm ss}+1}.
L000269	\end{equation}
L000270	This condition satisfies the form of a thermal state characterized by the average occupation number $n_{\rm ss}$.
L000271	
L000272	\vspace{0em}
L000273	\subsection*{Fitting constraints for phonon-number distribution} \label{app_constraint}
L000274	\vspace{0em}
L000275	
L000276	Extreme outliers in $p_n$ from free-parameter fits to Eq.~(6) in the End Matter \cite{so2025fT} can affect the accuracy of the estimate of $\braket{n(t)}$. To mitigate this, we first estimate a thermal mean occupation number $n_{\rm ave}$ by fitting the dynamics to:
L000277	\begin{equation}
L000278	    P_{\uparrow} (t,t_{\rm p})=\frac{1}{2}\sum_n p_n^{\rm th}(t)[1-e^{-\gamma t_{\rm p}}\cos{( \Omega t_{\rm p}\sqrt{n+1})}],
L000279	    \label{eq_thermal}
L000280	\end{equation}
L000281	where $p_n^{\rm th}\equiv\frac{n_{\rm ave}^n}{(n_{\rm ave}+1)^{n+1}}$. We then use $p_n^{\rm th}$ to weakly constrain the free-parameter fits by enforcing $p_n<c\times p_n^{\rm th}$, where the constraint factor $c$ is chosen between 50 and 500. These constraints are relevant only at very low temperatures ($n_{\rm ss}<1$); at higher temperatures, the fitting parameters are essentially unconstrained. We also compare the fitted populations $p_n$ to the thermal distribution $p_n^{\rm th}$ to confirm that, after being in contact with the engineered bath for a sufficiently long time, the motional mode reaches a thermal steady state (see End Matter). We note that at elevated temperatures ($n_{\rm ss}\gtrsim 4$), phonon-population measurements using the blue-sideband drive are more susceptible to systematic fluctuations in the probe dynamics, such as those caused by laser power and trap-frequency instabilities. These effects can lead to inaccurate estimations of $p_n$, particularly for large $n$, and thus of $n_{\rm ss}$.
